      "worked": "Since $(x+1)^2\\ge0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=|(x+1)^2(x-2)|\\end{align*}$$\nSketch the base cubic $y=(x+1)^2(x-2)$. It touches the $x$-axis at the double root $x=-1$, crosses at $x=2$, and has $y$-intercept $-2$. It is negative for $x<2$, apart from the zero at $x=-1$.\n\nReflect that negative section upwards and retain the section on $x\\ge2$. The double-root contact at $(-1,0)$ stays smooth, while $(2,0)$ becomes a sharp minimum. The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2)$, and both tails rise without bound.\n\nDomain: $\\mathbb R$. Range: $[0,\\infty)$.",
